<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Anglo-Sikh War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1845">1845</a>–<a href="/years/1846">1846</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> War was fought in 1845–1846 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> and the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalsa">Khalsa</a> army fought hard but was defeated, partly owing to the manoeuvres of its own leaders. The peace treaty ceded territory, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, and placed the Sikh state under British influence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-anglo-sikh-war.jpg" alt="First Anglo-Sikh War" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_Anglo-Sikh_War_Collage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Maharaja Ranjit Singh</a> expanded and consolidated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> in the early nineteenth century, as British-controlled territories advanced to its borders. He kept a policy of wary friendship with the British while building up his army with American and European mercenaries as trainers. After his death in 1839 the kingdom fell into disorder: his successors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharak-singh">Kharak Singh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nau-nihal-singh">Nau Nihal Singh</a> died within months in suspicious circumstances, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-singh">Sher Singh</a> was murdered in September 1843. The army grew from 29,000 men in 1839 to over 80,000 in 1845, and its regimental committees formed an alternative source of power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jind-kaur">Jind Kaur</a> became regent for her infant son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duleep-singh">Duleep Singh</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lal-singh">Lal Singh</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and Tej Singh as army commander.</p><p>The British East India Company had meanwhile built up its strength near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, with a cantonment at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firozpur">Firozpur</a>, and annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> in 1843. Most British accounts say their concern was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> army's threat to the border; Sikh and Indian historians say that preparations such as bridging trains and siege batteries show an intent to conquer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>After diplomatic relations broke, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> army began crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a> on 11 December 1845, claiming to enter Sikh possessions; the British saw it as hostile and declared war. They won an untidy battle at Mudki on 18 December. At Ferozeshah on 21 December the Sikh artillery caused heavy British casualties, and Hardinge expected defeat, but the British rallied the next morning. Sikh accounts say that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lal-singh">Lal Singh</a> held back the irregular cavalry and that Tej Singh withdrew when victory was near.</p><p>On 28 January 1846 Sir Harry Smith won at Aliwal. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sobraon">Sobraon</a> on 10 February the British broke through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> entrenchments, and the pontoon bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a> broke, trapping nearly 20,000 Sikh troops on the east bank. Sikh accounts say Tej Singh deliberately weakened it; British accounts say it broke under the weight of retreating men. None of the trapped soldiers tried to surrender, and the Sikhs lost between 8,000 and 10,000 men.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lahore">Treaty of Lahore</a> on 9 March 1846 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> ceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bist-doab">Jullundur Doab</a> and owed an indemnity of 15 million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>. Unable to pay, the Durbar ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, Hazarah and the hill country between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beas">Beas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulab-singh">Gulab Singh</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> bought Kashmir from the Company for 7.5 million rupees under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-amritsar-1846">Treaty of Amritsar</a>. The Treaty of Bhyroval of 16 December 1846 replaced the regent with a British resident, giving the Company control of the government. Resentment led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-sikh-war">Second Anglo-Sikh War</a> within three years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Anglo-Sikh War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>First Anglo-Sikh War</b> (1845–1846) ended in Sikh defeat at Sobraon; the Treaty of Lahore stripped the empire of the Jullundur Doab and Kashmir, which the British then sold to the Dogra Gulab Singh for 7.5 million rupees under the Treaty of Amritsar (1846).</blockquote><a href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>First Anglo-Sikh War</b> in 1846 the British confirmed the Rajas in their state.</blockquote><a href="/state/chamba-state">Chamba State</a></li><li><blockquote>Raja Pahar Singh sided with the British in the <b>First Anglo-Sikh War</b> in 1845 and was rewarded with land.</blockquote><a href="/state/faridkot-state">Faridkot State</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>First Anglo-Sikh War</b>, the British sold him the Kashmir valley by the Treaty of Amritsar in 1846, creating a large state under the Dogra dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/jammu-and-kashmir-princely-state">Jammu and Kashmir (princely state)</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>First Anglo-Sikh War</b> it passed to the British in 1846 and became part of Kangra district; the royal family kept only the jagir of Rupi.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kullu">Kingdom of Kullu</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>First Anglo-Sikh War</b> of 1845–46 Ajit Singh joined the Sikh army and took part in the campaign around Buddowal and Aliwal.</blockquote><a href="/state/ladwa-state">Ladwa State</a></li></ul></section>
